<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
                           xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.1"
                           schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">

  <displayName>Printo Agent</displayName>
  <description>Group Policy settings for the Printo virtual printer agent.</description>

  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_PrintoAgent_1">Printo Agent 0.1 or later</string>

      <string id="Printo">Printo</string>
      <string id="Agent">Agent</string>

      <string id="ServerUrl">Server address</string>
      <string id="ServerUrl_Explain">Base address of the Printo server, for example https://printo.example.local/api/.

The agent uses this to enrol, to download the routing rule bundle, and - in Server and Auto decision modes - to ask the server where each page should go.

Only measured page features cross the network, never the document itself: the workstation stays the only place a customer's invoice is rendered.

If this policy is Not Configured, the agent uses the address the MSI was installed with, or the address in agent.json. If none of those is set, the agent runs standalone on the routing rules it shipped with.</string>

      <string id="DecisionMode">Decision mode</string>
      <string id="DecisionMode_Explain">How the agent decides where each page goes.

Local: everything is decided on the workstation from the cached rule bundle. Works with no server at all.

Server: the page features are sent to the server, which returns a per-page plan. If the server cannot be reached, the document is held and the operator is asked rather than guessed at.

Auto (recommended): local first. A confident answer never touches the network; anything below the confidence threshold is escalated to the server. If the server is unreachable, a confident local answer still prints and is recorded as having run on cached rules.

If this policy is Not Configured, the agent uses Auto.</string>
      <string id="DecisionMode_Local">Local - decide on the workstation</string>
      <string id="DecisionMode_Server">Server - decide on the server</string>
      <string id="DecisionMode_Auto">Auto - local first, escalate when uncertain (recommended)</string>

      <string id="ConfidenceThreshold">Confidence threshold</string>
      <string id="ConfidenceThreshold_Explain">How confident the agent must be to route a page without asking, between 0 and 1. The default is 0.75.

In Auto mode this is the point at which a document is escalated to the server. In Local mode it is the point at which the operator is shown the picker.

Lower it and fewer documents interrupt an operator, but more pages are routed on weaker evidence. Raise it and routing is more cautious, at the cost of more prompts. The fleet console reports, per fallback reason, how often operators simply agreed with what the engine had already proposed - that number is the evidence for changing this.

A value outside 0 to 1 is ignored rather than clamped, and the agent falls back to the layer below.</string>

      <string id="EnrollmentToken">Enrolment token</string>
      <string id="EnrollmentToken_Explain">A token issued from the fleet console that lets a machine register itself with the server.

Set this to a multi-use token to enrol a fleet unattended: each machine reads it once on first start, exchanges it for its own per-machine credential, and never needs it again.

The token is not a long-lived secret - it only permits enrolment, it expires, and its use count is enforced by the server. The credential it buys is stored on the workstation with an ACL that excludes ordinary users.

Clear this policy once the fleet has enrolled.</string>

      <string id="VirtualPrinterEnabled">Virtual printer</string>
      <string id="VirtualPrinterEnabled_Explain">Whether the agent presents a virtual printer to Windows applications on this machine.

Enabled (the default): the agent creates a Windows print queue and captures everything printed to it. This is how documents normally reach Printo - the operator presses Ctrl+P and picks the Printo printer, exactly as they did with Print&amp;Share.

Disabled: no queue is created and the endpoint does not listen. Watched folders become the only way documents reach the agent, which is what a site wants when it feeds Printo from an ERP export directory rather than from people printing.

Turning this off does not remove a queue that already exists; use the Printo Settings window or run Printo.Agent.exe --remove-virtual-printer.</string>

      <string id="VirtualPrinterName">Virtual printer name</string>
      <string id="VirtualPrinterName_Explain">The name the queue appears under in the print dialog. The default is Printo.

Change it and the agent creates a queue under the new name at its next start. The old queue is left in place: removing a printer an operator may have set as their default is not something a policy refresh should do silently.

Use the same name across the fleet. Anything that prints to Printo by name - a browser kiosk profile, a shortcut, an ERP print setting - is naming this.</string>

      <string id="VirtualPrinterPort">Virtual printer port</string>
      <string id="VirtualPrinterPort_Explain">The loopback TCP port the agent's IPP endpoint listens on. The default is 39631.

The endpoint binds 127.0.0.1 only, so nothing outside the workstation can reach it and no firewall rule is needed.

Change this only if another program on the workstation already uses the port. The Windows queue records the port in its port name, so after a change the agent recreates the queue - which briefly removes and re-adds the printer.</string>

      <string id="VirtualPrinterManageQueue">Manage the Windows queue</string>
      <string id="VirtualPrinterManageQueue_Explain">Whether the agent creates and repairs the Windows print queue itself.

Enabled (the default): at startup, and every fifteen minutes after, the agent checks that the queue exists and points at its endpoint, and creates or repairs it if not. A printer somebody deleted comes back on its own.

Disabled: the endpoint still listens, but the queue is left entirely to the administrator. Choose this if printers are deployed by Group Policy and an agent creating one locally would fight the deployment. Create the queue with:

    Add-Printer -Name Printo -IppURL http://127.0.0.1:39631/ipp/print

run while the agent service is running - Windows reads the printer's capabilities before it will bind a queue.</string>

      <string id="OcrLanguage">OCR language</string>
      <string id="OcrLanguage_Explain">Language tag for the Windows OCR recogniser, for example en-US or pl-PL.

Some courier documents carry no text layer, so a rule has to read the page. If no recogniser is available the agent asks the operator rather than guessing, so this is about reducing prompts, not about correctness.

If this policy is Not Configured, the agent uses the machine's own display language. The matching Windows OCR language pack must be installed for a language to be usable.</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_PrintoAgent_2">Printo Agent 0.1.15 or later</string>
      <string id="WaybillHandling">Waybill copies</string>
      <string id="WaybillHandling_Explain">Where the carrier's own copy of the waybill prints: the DHL courier sheet ("WAYBILL DOC - Hand to Courier") and the FedEx AWB copy.

Route normally: the routing rules decide - the DHL sheet on A4, the FedEx AWB copy on the thermal printer with the labels. This is what every agent did before this setting existed.

Always A4 / Always thermal: every waybill copy goes to that printer; on thermal it is cropped like a label.

Do not print: waybill copies are left out of every job; the rest of the document prints, and the agent's history records the pages as not printed.

Any choice but Route normally makes the agent read each FedEx 4x6in label (OCR), because the AWB copy can only be told from the label by what it says.

If this policy is Not Configured, the agent uses its own setting, or the fleet policy from the Printo server.</string>
      <string id="WaybillHandling_Route">Route normally</string>
      <string id="WaybillHandling_A4">Always print them on A4</string>
      <string id="WaybillHandling_Thermal">Always print them on the thermal printer</string>
      <string id="WaybillHandling_Skip">Do not print them</string>
      <string id="ThermalMedia">Thermal media</string>
      <string id="ThermalMedia_Explain">The label stock loaded in thermal printers that do not name their own, as width x height in millimetres - for example 100x210mm, the product default, or 100x150mm.

A printer's own media, set in the agent's Printers settings, always wins over this.

If this policy is Not Configured, the agent uses its own setting, the fleet policy from the Printo server, or 100x210mm.</string>
      <string id="LogToFile">Write log files</string>
      <string id="LogToFile_Explain">Whether the agent and its tray write log files in the agent's data directory (C:\ProgramData\Printo\agent\logs by default), in addition to the Windows event log.

Files rotate by size and only the newest are kept (see the file size and file count policies), so they never take more than size x count.

Enabled: log files are written. Disabled: they are not. Not Configured: the agent uses its own setting or the fleet policy; the product default is off.</string>
      <string id="LogLevel">Log file level</string>
      <string id="LogLevel_Explain">The least severe entry written to the log files: Debug writes everything, including each job's full audit trail; Critical writes only failures that stop the agent working.

If this policy is Not Configured, the agent uses its own setting, the fleet policy, or Information.</string>
      <string id="LogLevel_Debug">Debug</string>
      <string id="LogLevel_Information">Information</string>
      <string id="LogLevel_Warning">Warning</string>
      <string id="LogLevel_Error">Error</string>
      <string id="LogLevel_Critical">Critical</string>
      <string id="LogMaxFileSizeMb">Log file size (MB)</string>
      <string id="LogMaxFileSizeMb_Explain">A log file is closed and a new one started when it reaches this size. Not Configured: 10 MB unless the agent or fleet policy says otherwise.</string>
      <string id="LogMaxFiles">Log files to keep</string>
      <string id="LogMaxFiles_Explain">How many log files are kept, the current one included; older ones are deleted. Not Configured: 5 unless the agent or fleet policy says otherwise.</string>
      <string id="KeepPrintedHours">Keep printed documents (hours)</string>
      <string id="KeepPrintedHours_Explain">How long the agent keeps its copy of a document after printing it. 0 removes it as soon as the job is finished. Not Configured: 24 hours unless the agent or fleet policy says otherwise.

Every document is copied into the spool before it is printed, so nothing is lost if the machine stops mid-job; this keeps that copy from accumulating.</string>
      <string id="KeepHistoryDays">Keep job history (days)</string>
      <string id="KeepHistoryDays_Explain">How long finished jobs stay in the agent's history and status window. Not Configured: 14 days unless the agent or fleet policy says otherwise.</string>
      <string id="ExpireUnprintedDays">Give up on unprinted jobs (days)</string>
      <string id="ExpireUnprintedDays_Explain">Jobs that failed, or are waiting for someone to answer the page picker, are cancelled and their documents removed after this many days. Jobs not yet looked at are never expired. Not Configured: 30 days unless the agent or fleet policy says otherwise.</string>
      <string id="MaxSpoolMb">Spool size limit (MB)</string>
      <string id="MaxSpoolMb_Explain">A ceiling on the documents the agent keeps. Past it, documents of finished jobs are removed oldest first; work that has not printed is never removed to make room. Not Configured: 2048 MB unless the agent or fleet policy says otherwise.</string>
      <string id="UsersCanControlService">Let users start and stop the agent</string>
      <string id="UsersCanControlService_Explain">Whether people signed in at the workstation may start, stop and restart the Printo Agent service - from the Printo window, the tray menu, or after saving settings.

Enabled or Not Configured: interactive users may start and stop it (and nothing else: they cannot reconfigure the service). Disabled: only administrators may; the tray asks for administrator approval instead.

The agent applies this each time it starts.</string>
    </stringTable>

    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="ServerUrl">
        <textBox refId="ServerUrl_Text">
          <label>Server address</label>
          <defaultValue>https://printo.example.local/api/</defaultValue>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="DecisionMode">
        <dropdownList refId="DecisionMode_Enum" defaultItem="2">Decision mode</dropdownList>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="ConfidenceThreshold">
        <textBox refId="ConfidenceThreshold_Text">
          <label>Threshold (0 to 1)</label>
          <defaultValue>0.75</defaultValue>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="EnrollmentToken">
        <textBox refId="EnrollmentToken_Text">
          <label>Token</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="OcrLanguage">
        <textBox refId="OcrLanguage_Text">
          <label>Language tag</label>
          <defaultValue>en-US</defaultValue>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="VirtualPrinterName">
        <textBox refId="VirtualPrinterName_Text">
          <label>Printer name</label>
          <defaultValue>Printo</defaultValue>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="VirtualPrinterPort">
        <decimalTextBox refId="VirtualPrinterPort_Decimal" defaultValue="39631">Loopback port</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>


      <presentation id="WaybillHandling">
        <dropdownList refId="WaybillHandling_Enum" defaultItem="0">Waybill copies</dropdownList>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="ThermalMedia">
        <textBox refId="ThermalMedia_Text">
          <label>Width x height</label>
          <defaultValue>100x210mm</defaultValue>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="LogLevel">
        <dropdownList refId="LogLevel_Enum" defaultItem="1">Level</dropdownList>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="LogMaxFileSizeMb">
        <decimalTextBox refId="LogMaxFileSizeMb_Decimal" defaultValue="10">Megabytes</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="LogMaxFiles">
        <decimalTextBox refId="LogMaxFiles_Decimal" defaultValue="5">Files</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="KeepPrintedHours">
        <decimalTextBox refId="KeepPrintedHours_Decimal" defaultValue="24">Hours</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="KeepHistoryDays">
        <decimalTextBox refId="KeepHistoryDays_Decimal" defaultValue="14">Days</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="ExpireUnprintedDays">
        <decimalTextBox refId="ExpireUnprintedDays_Decimal" defaultValue="30">Days</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="MaxSpoolMb">
        <decimalTextBox refId="MaxSpoolMb_Decimal" defaultValue="2048">Megabytes</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
